[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Strings: Pattern Matching (Naive, KMP, Boyer-Moore)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pattern matching, also known as string searching, is a fundamental problem in computer science. It involves finding occurrences of a specific pattern (a short string) within a larger text (a long string). This tutorial explores three common algorithms for pattern matching: the Naive algorithm, the Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) algorithm, and the Boyer-Moore algorithm."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Naive Pattern Matching"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Naive algorithm is the simplest approach to pattern matching. It involves sliding the pattern across the text, one character at a time, and comparing the pattern with the corresponding substring of the text. If a mismatch is found, the pattern is shifted by one position, and the comparison restarts."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"def naive_pattern_matching(text, pattern):
  n = len(text)
  m = len(pattern)
  occurrences = []
  for i in range(n - m + 1):
    j = 0
    while j < m and text[i + j] == pattern[j]:
      j += 1
    if j == m:
      occurrences.append(i)
  return occurrences"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The time complexity of the Naive algorithm is O(m*n) in the worst case, where 'n' is the length of the text and 'm' is the length of the pattern. This occurs when the pattern almost matches the text at every position."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) Algorithm"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The KMP algorithm is a more efficient pattern matching algorithm that avoids unnecessary comparisons by pre-processing the pattern to create a 'longest proper prefix suffix' (LPS) array. This array helps to determine how much to shift the pattern when a mismatch occurs."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"def compute_lps_array(pattern):
  m = len(pattern)
  lps = [0] * m
  length = 0  # Length of the previous longest prefix suffix
  i = 1
  while i < m:
    if pattern[i] == pattern[length]:
      length += 1
      lps[i] = length
      i += 1
    else:
      if length != 0:
        length = lps[length - 1]
      else:
        lps[i] = 0
        i += 1
  return lps

def kmp_pattern_matching(text, pattern):
  n = len(text)
  m = len(pattern)
  lps = compute_lps_array(pattern)
  i = 0  # index for text
  j = 0  # index for pattern
  occurrences = []
  while i < n:
    if pattern[j] == text[i]:
      i += 1
      j += 1
    if j == m:
      occurrences.append(i - j)
      j = lps[j - 1]
    elif i < n and pattern[j] != text[i]:
      if j != 0:
        j = lps[j - 1]
      else:
        i += 1
  return occurrences"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The time complexity of the KMP algorithm is O(n) in the worst case, where 'n' is the length of the text. The pre-processing step to compute the LPS array takes O(m) time, where 'm' is the length of the pattern."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Boyer-Moore Algorithm"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Boyer-Moore algorithm is another efficient pattern matching algorithm that uses two heuristics to improve performance: the 'bad character' heuristic and the 'good suffix' heuristic. These heuristics allow the algorithm to skip more characters in the text when a mismatch occurs."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"def compute_bad_character_table(pattern):
  m = len(pattern)
  bad_char = {}
  for i in range(m):
    bad_char[pattern[i]] = i
  return bad_char

def boyer_moore_pattern_matching(text, pattern):
  n = len(text)
  m = len(pattern)
  bad_char = compute_bad_character_table(pattern)
  occurrences = []
  s = 0
  while s <= (n - m):
    j = m - 1
    while j >= 0 and pattern[j] == text[s + j]:
      j -= 1
    if j < 0:
      occurrences.append(s)
      s += (m - bad_char[text[s + m]] if (s + m) < n and text[s+m] in bad_char else 1)
    else:
      s += max(1, j - (bad_char[text[s + j]] if text[s+j] in bad_char else -1))
  return occurrences"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The time complexity of the Boyer-Moore algorithm can vary depending on the specific implementation and the characteristics of the text and pattern. In the best case, it can achieve a time complexity of O(n/m), where 'n' is the length of the text and 'm' is the length of the pattern. However, in the worst case, its time complexity can be O(m*n), similar to the Naive algorithm. However, in practice, Boyer-Moore often performs very well."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, while the Naive algorithm is simple to understand, KMP and Boyer-Moore offer significant performance improvements, especially for larger texts and patterns. The choice of algorithm depends on the specific application and the expected characteristics of the input data."}]
